    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d30">

                <pb facs="0148" n="117" sameAs="frus:frus1969-76v31/pg117"/>

                <head>30. Backchannel Message From the President’s Assistant for National Security
                    Affairs (Kissinger) to Secretary of the Treasury Shultz in Paris<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d30-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1973-03-10T15:50:00+00:00">March 10, 1973,
                        1550Z</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>WH30719. Following is a text of a letter from Brandt to the President which has
                    just been delivered.</p>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Begin text.</hi> Bonn, March 8, 1973. Dear Mr.
                    President:</p>
                <p>Thank you very much for your letter of 5 March.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d30-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>In my letter of 2 March<anchor n="3" xml:id="d30-f3"/> I did not, of course,
                    advocate any solution to the currency problems that could impair Atlantic
                    cooperation. I did not and do not hold any such view. You know from the <pb
                        facs="0149" n="118" xml:id="pg118"/> developments of the last few days and
                    from the contacts between Secretary Shultz, Dr. Kissinger and Minister Schmidt
                    that my efforts have been devoted to finding a solution which does justice to
                    all the Federal Republic’s international commitments. Mr. Shultz and Herr
                    Schmidt will, I am informed, meet in Paris on Friday morning,<anchor n="4"
                        xml:id="d30-f4"/> and Mr. Shultz will be meeting me next week in Bonn. This
                    will give us the opportunity to discuss, in the atmosphere of frankness and
                    mutual confidence usual at our meetings, the problems which have arisen.</p>
                <p>I am confident that we can find solutions which strengthen both European
                    integration and Atlantic partnership.</p>
                <p>With best wishes, sgd. Willy Brandt <hi rendition="#italic">End text</hi>
                </p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d30-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d30-f1-content">
                <p>Source: National Archives, Nixon Presidential Materials, NSC Files, Box 953, VIP
                    Visits, George P. Shultz (Europe &amp; USSR), March 8–22, 1973 [&amp;
                    September–October]. Confidential; Immediate; Sensitive; Exclusively Eyes Only;
                    Urgent. The message was sent to Shultz through the Embassy in Paris.</p>
            </note>
